  "text": "**Solution 2 is wrong.** The ideal $(x, y^2, z)$ is generated by a regular sequence of powers of variables, so its Rees algebra is a polynomial ring over $k$ and every power is integrally closed. It is a normal ideal, so it cannot serve as a counterexample for $N = 3$.\n\n**Solution 3 is not reliable.** Its 4-generator argument is only asserted (\"through systematic analysis...\"), and its claim that non-normal examples need at least 5 generators is never established. Its answer of 3 rests on that unproven step.\n\n**Solution 1 is plausible.** I recall a Reid–Roberts–Vitulli-type example, $(x^4, x^3y, xy^3, y^4, x^2y^2z)$, which is integrally closed but not normal. It has height 2, so adding $z^c$ gives a height-3 integrally closed ideal with 6 minimal generators that fails to be normal. That fits the claim that 6 generators can fail and 5 cannot. I could not verify the exact paper or the claim that all height-3 integrally closed monomial ideals with at most 5 generators are normal. The citation in Solution 1 looks doubtful, so its answer of 5 is supported by this example but not confirmed by the cited source.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
